  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nWe have sounded like the broken record for the past week and I am \nsorry, but still no changes. An area of high pressure remains \ncentered near the Four Corners region, meandering south-southeast \nthrough the week, keeping us hot and dry through the end of the \nweek. There is a very low chance of some seabreeze convection in the \nafternoons, though this is pretty slim. Guidance is still hinting at \nthe potential for a disturbance to sneak around the eastern \nperiphery of the ridge around the Friday timeframe. This could lead \nto some isolated showers and slightly cooler temperatures but, \nagain, this is still pretty far out so we are not getting our hopes \nup.\n\nThanks to the presence of below normal moisture today and tomorrow \nwhich will help keep heat index values slightly below 110 for most \nof South Texas. Will maintain not issuing a heat advisory today, \nthough will monitor for a short-fused advisory tomorrow. Moisture \nwill begin to gradual return Wednesday through the end of the week, \nresulting in higher heat index values once again. Heat Advisories \nwill likely be needed Wednesday through the end of the week. \nRegardless of the slightly lower heat index values, we urge you all \nto be extremely cautious if you are outside. Cumulative heat stress \ncan lead to increased chances for heat exhaustion or heat stroke. \nRemember to take breaks in the shade or air conditioning, drink \nwater/electrolytes, and pace yourself if working outside. Don't \nleave children or pets in your vehicle, even for short periods of \ntime.",
